[cot:thought] Sufficient data exists to build a solid data module. Extracted key figures
[cot:thought] for the post. KFF reports $27.5B in gross Part D GLP-1 spending for 2024. Fonteum prescriber data totals $24.57B for the GLP-1 class.
[cot:thought] Total Part D spending diverges between Fonteum ($226.74B) and MedPAC ($288.9B) due to different denominators. Ozempic leads at $12.38B and 2M enrollees. Mounjaro reaches roughly $5.83B with about 1M enrollees.
[cot:thought] KFF claims climbed from 4.8M in 2019 to 21.8M in 2024. MedPAC estimates diabetic
[cot:thought] therapies at roughly 20% of Part D, with injectable antidiabetics and oral agents combining for about 12.1% and 8.8% respectively.
[cot:thought] Enrollment concentration by plan is the chosen proxy for drug-level spend, since public per-plan GLP-1 spending is unavailable. UnitedHealth carries 12.6 million enrollees at 23%, while Centene, CVS, and Humana each hold about 15%, leaving the top five plans at 73.5%. MA-PD enrollment exceeds PDP, though per-enrollee spend is nearly identical across both segments.
[cot:thought] KFF brand shares are
[cot:thought] now sharper. Ozempic accounts for 47% of gross spending. Mounjaro holds 23%. Rybelsus contributes 7%. Wegovy contributes 1%. The remaining share belongs to Trulicity and older agents. The 2019 baseline is roughly $5.5B. The full data module is being drafted and committed.
